gm-method debugging lesson (CPU-collider vs GPU-rendered terrain mismatch on a procedural-fractal planet): when a CPU-computed height (physics collider, veg/grass placement) visibly FLOATS/SINKS vs the GPU-rendered surface, and the height fns are 'identical source', the cause is often FLOAT PRECISION: the GPU runs the fractal in float32 (GLSL highp single) while the CPU JS transpile runs it in float64 (double). A many-octave fBm (e.g. 46 octaves) ACCUMULATES the f32-vs-f64 difference into a multi-metre gap. WITNESS the cause by ELIMINATION, and beware false 'it matches' readings: (1) comparing the CPU height fn to ANOTHER CPU projection of itself is CPU-vs-CPU and trivially matches 0 - it does NOT prove CPU==GPU; you must compare against the ACTUAL GPU shader output. (2) The GPU height probe (a 1px _PROBE_ readback like sampleGroundM) is often ASYNC/1-frame-stale/frame-coupled - the SDK may document it as 'NOT a tight oracle'; drive it RELIABLY by hammering the SAME input until the returned value STABILIZES (same value twice) and confirm DISTINCT inputs give DISTINCT outputs (not all the stale same). (3) Confirm the precision hypothesis by grep -c 'fround' in the CPU glsl-runtime: zero = it computes in float64. THE FIX: wrap the CPU glsl-runtime scalar ops (the _bin/_u chokepoint: add/sub/mul/div + unary) in Math.fround so the JS fractal sums in float32 matching the GPU; CAVEAT GLSL may use FMA/wider intermediates so per-op fround is not guaranteed EXACT - re-measure the GPU-vs-CPU gap after and only ship if it drops to ~0, then re-bless the golden (it WILL change) + publish. Eliminate the cheap suspects FIRST (uniforms via gl.getUniform, HPF seed/res, the local->dir mapping by passing the SAME dir to both, staleness) before blaming precision. A debug-DRAW of the collider (wireframe at the height-fn samples) is the user's reliable witness when headless pixel-readback lies on a preserveDrawingBuffer:false canvas.